          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Where does guidance differ on non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI?",
          "answer": "The search results don't answer this question. The uncomplicated UTI guideline is listed as having a section on where guidance differs, but the passage I received covers only who the guidance applies to and when to test without a urine culture. It doesn't describe any disagreement about non-antibiotic treatment.\n\nThe results did include two related points from the UTI guidance (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance, \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection\"):\n- Empiric treatment without a culture is appropriate for typical symptoms when there is no vaginal discharge or irritation.\n- A urine dipstick adds little in that group. It is more useful when symptoms are unclear.\n\nA passage on delayed prescribing appeared in the results, but it comes from the guidance on upper respiratory infections, not UTI, so I can't use it here.\n\nTo find the actual points of disagreement, you could look up the full text of the uncomplicated UTI guideline, or ask a clinician or pharmacist, who can also advise on what options suit a particular situation.\n\nIf you have symptoms now, such as fever, back or side pain, vomiting, confusion, or blood in the urine, contact a clinician promptly. Those can signal a more serious infection.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
